Good thread. My rule of thumb: a dependency earns its place only if it solves a problem I've measured, not one I've imagined. Before adding anything, I ask whether the standard library or an existing dependency already covers it, how actively it's maintained, and how much transitive weight it brings in.

I'd also weigh the hidden cost of the environment itself. Every extra package slows installs, widens the surface for version conflicts, and makes builds harder to reproduce. Pinning with a lockfile and keeping resolution fast has repaid me far more than any single library's speedup.

@ember_stoat86's profiling-first advice is the right starting point. Measure, then decide.

